Yes. The paradox resolves if the “sameness” is not sameness of people, but sameness of interface.

A shared language, protocol, rule set, coordinate system, value, or invariant can reduce communication cost so much that people are free to differ more everywhere else.

\text{shared invariant} \uparrow
\;\Rightarrow\;
\text{coordination friction} \downarrow
\;\Rightarrow\;
\text{safe diversity elsewhere} \uparrow

That is why common standards often increase variety rather than reduce it. A musical key lets very different instruments improvise together. A network protocol lets radically different computers communicate. Shared grammar lets people say novel things. Traffic rules permit thousands of different destinations without everyone crashing into one another.

So the architecture is not:

\text{sameness everywhere}

but:

\boxed{\text{sameness where coordination requires it}+\text{freedom everywhere else}}

Your Ariadne thread works well as the metaphor for the invariant. The maze can be extraordinarily complicated. Everyone can take different turns, explore different rooms, find different things. But if they retain a trustworthy connection to the thread, they preserve a route back to shared orientation.

And once you recognize that another person is carrying the same thread, you don’t have to demand that they occupy your location in the maze. You can infer:

Different route, same orienting constraint.

That creates room for genuine pluralism without pure relativism. People can disagree about local maps while still sharing enough structure to cooperate.

The “do unto others” piece fits as a reciprocity invariant. You do not need identical preferences or identities for reciprocity to work. You need the shared recognition that the other person’s perspective has the same kind of standing that yours does.

So:

\text{I am a perspective}

\text{you are a perspective}

\text{neither perspective exhausts the maze}

\text{shared thread lets us coordinate anyway}

There is an important caution, though: the thread has to remain identifiable and revisable. If people start confusing one historical expression of the thread with the invariant itself, then the common standard can become conformity.

So the stronger principle is:

Preserve the minimum shared structure necessary for mutual intelligibility; maximize legitimate variation above it.

That is a very general design rule—for families, teams, societies, software, science, ecosystems of ideas, and probably a large portion of what we’ve been calling the Pantheon.